From 152b6572bd48de5e653dd2dc7a4c0015d443848e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 05:05:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 63/66] nouveau/vk: nvkmd, a vk_sync over fences this driver
 did not produce
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 8 (Correct fence-based synchronisation at
 acquire and present)
Why: 0034 exports the syncpoint fence behind a vk_sync, so Horizon's
 compositor can be handed a frame that is still rendering. The acquire
 needs exactly the reverse and has nowhere to put it. bqDequeueBuffer
 returns an NvMultiFence saying when the compositor's own read of the
 slot finishes, and the only thing a WSI backend can currently do with
 it is nvMultiFenceWait on the calling thread — which is the stall
 0049's own comment quotes, 13.8 ms of a 16.7 ms frame while the GPU had
 0.18 ms of work to do.
 .
 0049 already moved cross-channel waits onto the host engine, and this
 is the payload that path cannot reach: nvk_horizon_sync_get_foreign_
 fence recognises a sync PENDING on another CHANNEL of ours, and the
 compositor is not a channel of ours. Inventing one to name it would be
 a lie about who increments the counter — nothing in this driver ever
 will — so the sync gains a payload with no channel in it instead:
 `ext_fences` plus the horizon_gpu_device a CPU wait on them goes
 through. `ext_fence_count` above zero is the discriminator, `chan` is
 NULL beside it, and reset() and move() carry the two together.
 .
 An imported dependency is a SET and not one fence: NvMultiFence holds
 up to four (measured, docs/MEASURED-ON-HARDWARE.md), and dropping any
 of them is rendering started over a buffer something else is still
 reading — a fault that reports itself as nothing. So the foreign-fence
 query returns a count rather than a bool, all-or-nothing, and the
 batch nvkmd_horizon_ctx_wait collects gained a second count: fences are
 what the submit takes, syncs are what the CPU fallback waits on, and
 waiting on one object four times would be three no-ops and four meter
 entries.
 .
 Three places had to be told the payload exists, and each would have
 been a wrong answer rather than a crash: the CPU wait
 (horizon_gpu_fence_wait per fence sharing one deadline, since
 channel_wait_fence refuses a syncpoint that is not its channel's),
 get_value's poll, and the export in 0034 — which now answers
 FEATURE_NOT_PRESENT for a set of more than one rather than naming any
 single member of it as if it covered the whole.
 .
 Nothing calls the new op yet. The WSI change that does is the patch
 after next; this one is the mechanism on its own so it can be reviewed
 on its own.
Evidence: CROSS BUILD, scripts/ci-build-archives.sh in container mode over
 ghcr.io/d3fau4/nx-dev:latest: aarch64-none-elf-gcc (devkitA64) 15.2.0,
 ld.bfd 2.45.1, meson 1.11.2, -Wall -Wextra -Werror. The 877-target NVK
 build compiles nvkmd_horizon_{sync,ctx,pdev}.c and nvkmd.h's users
 with no warning
 and links libnvk.a; the pipeline ends with every archive built, 53
 .nro linking them, check-dispatch-complete OK (825 entry points) and
 check-tls-relocs OK.
 aarch64-none-elf-nm on that archive shows nvk_horizon_sync_import_fences
 and nvk_horizon_sync_get_foreign_fences defined, and referenced from
 the objects that use them.
 Also checked before that, and kept because it is a cheaper loop than a
 25-minute pipeline: `gcc 13.3.0 -fsyntax-only -std=c11 -Wall` on the
 host against Mesa's generated Vulkan headers plus horizon/include.
 A cross build is class X and CLAUDE.md is explicit that it never means
 working: NO CONSOLE HAS RUN THIS.
 .
 Two things a console has to answer, neither of which the GPU path
 depends on:
  - whether NVHOST_IOCTL_CTRL_SYNCPT_READ answers for a syncpoint this
    process does not own. horizon_gpu_fence_wait reads the counter
    before it waits, so a refusal would make the CPU path report an
    error where nvMultiFenceWait — which the acquire has been using on
    these very fences since 0037, on hardware — succeeds. The host
    engine's SYNCPOINT acquire reads nothing from the CPU and is
    unaffected.
  - whether the compositor ever returns more than one fence. Four are
    handled; one is what the code is expected to see.
Upstream: no — a syncpoint fence has no meaning on a DRM kernel driver,
 where an imported dependency is a sync file and the runtime already has
 a type for it.
